so my pitch to you is that the problem  of democracy is actually that democracy  is one of the easiest forms of political  system to kind of hollow out from the  inside and as you said I've got a book  that's just come out called how to rig  an election when we started doing the  research for that book we thought we  were going to find relatively  significant problematic levels of  electoral manipulation around the world  but sort of 2025 percent what we  actually found is really striking  Stander's we're talking about something  like every other election that is being  held in the world today having  significant manipulation of one kind or  another we're talking about elections  that on average are such poor quality in  some parts of the world that were  actually seeing the opposition win only  one in ten elections that are organized  in other words elections that have  effectively stopped challenging  authoritarians  challenging poor leaders kicking the  bums out but actually served to  legitimate the existing distribution of  power those fantastic stories that we  find in the book of the way these  elections are being manipulated a great  one is for Madagascar where Marlo  Ravalomanana who's a very sort of famous  leader in the country a yogurt magnate  made his money in dairy he wanted to win  the election easily his opposition  leader was outside of the country he  decided that the best way to stop him  from actually competing in the election  were simply that every time he was  flying to the country keep in mind it's  an island he closed all of the airports  unilaterally right the guy never managed  to get into the country he couldn't  submit his papers he wasn't on the  ballot paper the election on the day was  pretty good quality why because you  don't need to worry about the election  on the day if you've excluded you're  most likely rival so on the day the  election was pretty free and fair what  happened international observers decided  that the election was good enough to get  a rubber stamp Ravalomanana not only  retained the presidency but with the  support of the international community  so my pitch here is the problem is not  so much democracy but that we have  allowed democracy to be hollowed out of  meaning and it's not just that this is  happening in far-flung tin-pot  dictatorships that you might think about  in other parts of the world post  communist Europe sub-saharan Africa Asia  Latin America where recently for example  we could talk about the rigging of the  election in Venezuela it's not just that  democracy is being hollowed out in those  places it's also at risk of being  hollowed out in the United States and  the United Kingdom as well the classic  example in the United States is it as  many of you will know we have seen  massive processes of gerrymandering  whereby essentially leaders get to pick  who votes for them rather than the other  way around  in other words we see places in the  United States where we actually get  people who are able to actually select  the people voting for them by  distributing constituency boundaries in  that way and what that means is that we  actually see an awful lot of elections  both in the UK in the US that are  effectively uncompetitive excluding  large portions of the electorate from  the political process so my argument is  that we don't need to abandon democracy  but we need a process of democratic  renewal  you
 

